**Replaces [decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) for date-report triage.**
The accepted record stays intact. This review uses the 4 October 2026 export;
its counts describe that snapshot, not the live support queue.

## What #4471 contains

The incident page says one issue and one queued fix. The table below runs the exported
classification code against distinct report IDs and tenant settings. It separates a shared
parser defect from a booking-form hint defect. Intake tags and browser locale are guesses;
the tenant's locale, form version, and the report's channel determine this classification.

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

### Shared month-first parser

The shared parser reads dates month first even for day-first tenants. App and email reports,
and reports through booking-v2, reach this cause. The same applies to non-form reports from
booking-v3 tenants. For Harbour Dental (`en-GB`, booking-v2), entering `07/04/2026` for
7 April produces 4 July. R-41200 records that reversal. Fix the parser to use the tenant's
specified date order, and reject ambiguous input when that order is unknown.

See the [parser ticket](https://github.com/remnov/mega-s01e03-support-kb/issues/1).
Its count follows the exported classifier, including the unresolved reports below.

### Booking-v3 prints the wrong hint

For a day-first tenant, booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY`, but the form reads the input in the
tenant's day-first order. Following the hint reverses month and day. This path requires
**channel `form`, booking-v3, and a day-first tenant**. Copper Studio, Alder Physio, and
Pale Harbour meet those tenant conditions. R-41230 is one example from Copper Studio.
Use a hint consistent with the tenant's parser and test the displayed hint and submitted date
together. Fixing the shared parser alone does not correct this form's instructions.

See the [form-hint ticket](https://github.com/remnov/mega-s01e03-support-kb/issues/2).

<Callout>
These are separate fixes. Matching words in a subject do not establish a shared cause.
</Callout>

## What the grouping does not prove

The classifier uses tenant configuration and channel; it does not inspect the report body.
The desk notes flag R-41202, R-41209, R-41236, R-41344, and R-41620. All remain counted under
`parser` by the exported rule. They are not proof of the parser defect: their bodies describe
one-day offsets, an invite arriving before its booking, or different days in different cities.
Keep them visible and investigate the original timestamp, tenant timezone, recipient timezone,
expected date, and actual date before declaring their cause resolved. Do not invent a confirmed
third defect from these symptoms or silently remove them from the count.

The raw export has overlapping pages. `uniqueById` counts each report ID once before filtering
for #4471; adding raw row counts would overstate the number of reports. The table's intake-tag
breakdown also exposes disagreements between intake's guess and the tenant-based cause.

## When reports arrived and when the desk merged them

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

Reports began arriving in April. The large merge bar in early July is a filing event,
not the start of the incident. Compare **received** with **merged** before calling a spike an
increase in failures. New reports continue after the bulk merge; “known” does not mean fixed.

## Replacement triage rule

1. Read the body. Record the tenant, channel, expected date, actual date, locale, and form version.
2. Look up the tenant's settings; do not substitute the reporter's browser locale or intake tag.
3. For the date paths supported by this export, use `causeOf` and compare with `sameCause`.
4. Merge only when the cause is known and matches. A matching subject alone is insufficient.
5. If the tenant is missing, the cause is unknown, or the symptom contradicts the assigned cause,
   keep the report for investigation. Preserve its original ID and evidence when moving it.
6. Count distinct report IDs. Link each cause to its own engineering ticket and check incoming
   reports again after a fix; do not mark the whole umbrella ticket resolved with one fix.

This trades the speed of subject-only matching for a cause the engineer can act on. It also
prevents different subject lines from splitting the same cause into duplicate tickets.

## Check the rule against examples

The explorer runs the same code as the cause table. Change a tenant or channel to see the
classification change. Subject edits alone cannot change the underlying cause.

<DedupeExplorer />

The examples show both failures of decision 0001: the same subject can hide different causes,
and different subjects can describe the same cause. Unknown tenants have no established cause;
they must not be auto-merged because their subjects happen to match.

## Review and ownership

Support should use this record in place of the old subject-only triage rule and the incident's
one-fix claim. Engineering owns the two fixes. Support should verify new reports against the
latest tenant configuration and re-render this page when its data changes. The layout marks
pages stale after the configured review interval; a recent review date does not make an old
export current. This page is a decision for this course exercise, not evidence that the live
support process or product has already changed.

